        "ballot_id": "B035",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "For the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B047",
    "B002"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattice clocks convert narrow atomic transitions into exceptionally accurate and stable time standards. Their independent realizations, sustained performance improvements, and demonstrated use in precision tests of fundamental physics make this a mature experimental achievement. Katori and Ye represent complementary foundational contributions, and a focused citation can recognize the clock advance without claiming the wider history of optical-clock development.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary remains important: a prize must distinguish the foundational contributions of Katori and Ye from later enabling work across a broad community. The clocks' precision and applications support the case, but do not by themselves settle attribution or the precise scope of the invention and realization.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that independent implementations and sustained precision gains establish optical lattice clocks as a mature method, while calling for historical review of the foundational contributions and their boundary with later advances.",
        "response": "I agree that independent implementations establish the method's maturity, and that historical review is still needed. The two independent foundational contributions support crediting both Katori and Ye; the citation should remain limited to the invention and realization of lattice clocks, without absorbing subsequent optical-clock advances."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene exposed experimentally established correlated phases in a tunable regime, while acknowledging that their relationships and mechanisms remain unsettled.",
        "response": "The new regime is compelling, and unresolved mechanisms do not erase the observed correlated behavior. I retain B002 as an alternative, but favor B035 because repeated clock realizations and established precision uses provide a longer record of experimental maturity and a more bounded method to recognize now."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical record should clarify the respective foundational contributions of Katori and Ye and distinguish them from later enabling advances.",
      "The relative weight of demonstrated precision tests and timekeeping in defining the clock achievement's scope remains open."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "My Chalmers work in electron microscopy overlaps professionally with B047's subject, which remains an alternative. The supplied profile identifies no personal or collaborator connection to its credited names."
  }
}
